<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Lu</h1><p class="st-meta">1042 BCE – 256 BCE (approx.) · capital Qufu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Shandong state founded for the Duke of Zhou's line, guardian of Zhou ritual, birthplace of Confucius and subject of the Spring and Autumn Annals.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a> was granted Lu but stayed at court as regent, sending his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bo-qin">Boqin</a> to govern it. Its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qufu">Qufu</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, was built on land associated with the earlier <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a> ally Yan, and Lu was charged with keeping order among the eastern peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>As the house of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-zhou">Duke of Zhou</a>, Lu was allowed to perform royal rites and kept the most complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou</a> ritual and musical traditions. Its court chronicle, the Spring and Autumn Annals, covering 722 to 481 BCE, gave its name to the era. Power slipped from the dukes to three ministerial families, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-huan">Jisun</a>, Mengsun and Shusun. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, born in Lu in 551 BCE, served briefly in its government.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Lu declined into a minor state during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> and was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-kaolie-of-chu">King Kaolie</a> in 256 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qufu">Qufu</a> remained the centre of the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a>, and the Temple and Cemetery of Confucius there are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-lu.jpg" alt="State of Lu" width="320" height="217"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Hirth · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sketch_Map_of_China_during_the_Chou_Dynasty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1042 BCE – 256 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Qufu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boqin, Duke Yin of Lu, Duke Ai of Lu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lu</dd></dl></aside></div>

